  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Loucas Haji-Ioannou was a Greek-Cypriot shipowner who founded Troodos Shipping and built a major private tanker fleet. His uncle's trading business provided early experience and commercial connections in Saudi Arabia. He subsequently developed his own shipping interests from London and Athens, earning the nickname 'Tanker King' through expansion in oil transport.",
    "html": "<p>Loucas Haji-Ioannou was a Greek-Cypriot shipowner who founded Troodos Shipping and built a major private tanker fleet. His uncle's trading business provided early experience and commercial connections in Saudi Arabia. He subsequently developed his own shipping interests from London and Athens, earning the nickname 'Tanker King' through expansion in oil transport.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 51,
    "confidence": {
      "score": 85,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Primary family history, firsthand formation and contemporary specialist reporting converge."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S5"
    ]
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Before owning a fleet, Loucas Haji-Ioannou learned shipping through the cement trade. After hearing that Greek producers wanted export customers, he contacted them by telegram and established an agency serving the ships carrying his imports. He studied two bills of lading closely to learn the contractual terms of the business.\n\nHe also created a lasting source of educational and medical funding. His endowment of the Loucas Haji-Ioannou Foundation supported scholarships for Cypriot students in financial need and grants for heart-disease research and treatment in Greece. The foundation was established in 1997 and subsequently chaired by his daughter Clelia.",
    "html": "<p>Before owning a fleet, Loucas Haji-Ioannou learned shipping through the cement trade. After hearing that Greek producers wanted export customers, he contacted them by telegram and established an agency serving the ships carrying his imports. He studied two bills of lading closely to learn the contractual terms of the business.</p>\r\n<p>He also created a lasting source of educational and medical funding. His endowment of the Loucas Haji-Ioannou Foundation supported scholarships for Cypriot students in financial need and grants for heart-disease research and treatment in Greece. The foundation was established in 1997 and subsequently chaired by his daughter Clelia.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 98,
